                    {
                      "type": "frame",
                      "id": "IWlAW",
                      "x": 232,
                      "y": 152,
                      "name": "ai-response",
                      "width": 768,
                      "layout": "vertical",
                      "gap": 12,
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "id": "rIb5s",
                          "name": "response-text",
                          "fill": "#FAFAFA",
                          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                          "width": "fill_container",
                          "content": "API rate limiting is a technique used to control the number of requests a client can make to an API within a specified time window. Here's a comprehensive overview:\n\nWhy Rate Limiting Matters:\nRate limiting protects your API from abuse, ensures fair usage among clients, prevents server overload, and helps manage costs associated with API calls.\n\nCommon Algorithms:\n\n1. Token Bucket — Tokens are added at a fixed rate. Each request consumes a token. If the bucket is empty, requests are rejected.\n\n2. Sliding Window — Tracks requests within a rolling time window, providing smoother rate enforcement than fixed windows.",
                          "lineHeight": 1.6,
                          "fontFamily": "Inter",
                          "fontSize": 14,
                          "fontWeight": "normal",
                          "letterSpacing": -0.084
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "frame",
                          "id": "SRCDV",
